@@ -0,0 +1,26 @@
+# Summary: in-repo parser replaces `inilite`
+
+## What changed
+
+- `confkit/parse.py`: the `inilite` import is gone. `loads()` and `load()` are now a full parser for `docs/FORMAT.md`: sections (dotted and spaced headers), dotted keys, quoted strings with escapes, multi-line values, comments, typed scalars (bool, decimal/hex/octal/binary int, float), lists (nested, trailing comma, comments), `@include` with cycle detection by real path, and all error kinds with line numbers and source.
+- `tests/test_format.py` (new): 40 tests for the spec's edge cases. No existing test was edited.
+- `vendor/`, `docs/`, `README.md` and the other files are unchanged.
+
+## What I verified
+
+- `python -m unittest`: 53 tests, all pass (13 existing, 40 new). Before the change it errored at import (`No module named 'inilite'`).
+- `python -m confkit <file>` prints JSON and exits 0. `--get server.port` prints `9000`. A missing `--get` key prints `no such key: ...` to stderr and exits 2. A missing argument prints usage and exits 2. A duplicate key prints `<path>:3: duplicate key: a.x` to stderr and exits 1.
+- Python 3.14.3, no network, no new dependencies.
+
+## Not verified / unsure
+
+- **Interpretation choices in the spec:**
+  - A section header needs whitespace before an inline comment: `[a]# c` is `invalid section header`.
+  - `@include"x"` (no space) is accepted. Only a name character directly after `@include` makes it `invalid line`.
+  - An unquoted list token may contain spaces: `[1 2]` is `["1 2"]`. "Two items without a comma" applies to quoted items and nested lists.
+  - A quoted string used as an item must close on its line (`unterminated string`).
+  - Included files also have a leading BOM stripped.
+- **Unspecified:** `load()` on a file that is not valid UTF-8 raises `UnicodeDecodeError`, not `ParseError`. The spec does not say what to do here.
+- **Test coverage:** the tests are my own reading of `docs/FORMAT.md`. A misreading would be shared by the code and the tests. I did not compare against the old `inilite` behavior, because `vendor/` may not be used.
+- **Not tested:** fuzzing, very large inputs, and Windows paths.
+- **Other notes:** the `test_format.py` file changed on disk after my last edit. I read it, and its content matches what I intended, so I left it as is.
